#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087443 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea23649-e835-540a-b238-7b89ed330cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "318fc39e-0efe-5b98-968c-ff4408706471" :question/math-academy-id "e-4502" :question/problem "Calculate $A^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = 1 \\cdot 1 - 0 \\cdot (-2) = 1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}]. \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -0 \\\\ -(-2) & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {A}^{-1}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087444 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "195d7dab-edf2-536b-a7b7-d1c16c7b1071" :question/math-academy-id "e-4503" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $A$, we have\n\n$$\n\\det({A})=1 \\cdot 1 - 1 \\cdot 1 = 0.\n$$\n\nBecause $\\det(A)=0,$ we conclude that $A$ is not invertible. Since $A$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $A$ is singular.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n\n$$\n\\det({C})=3 \\cdot 3 - (-3) \\cdot 3 = 18.\n$$\n\nBecause $\\det(C) \\neq 0,$ we conclude that $C$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ is singular."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087445 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable in a Singular Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "ef06dd89-501b-5d2b-a56d-4554a049a91d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4504" :question/problem "If \n$[\\begin{aligned}1-k & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-k\\end{aligned}]$\n is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ is singular if and only if $\\det(A)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(1-k)(1-k)-4 \\\\  & ={k}^{2}-2k-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}-2k-3 & =0 \\\\ (k-3)(k+1) & =0 \\\\ k & =3,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values of $k$ are $k=3$ and $k=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd25a02-4e2e-592b-b869-2889bf56d0f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ and $K$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277042} {:answer/id #uuid "a7780f8d-92ee-5bee-acda-ac38b221ba98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$J$ and $K$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277043} {:answer/id #uuid "d0033e22-6e20-53c9-b6ad-5502fdbe9286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ and $J$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277044} {:answer/id #uuid "66eb4339-b5c8-5b6f-a9c6-736fc2c5a548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277045} {:answer/id #uuid "bc62ee22-7c25-5746-b95d-31e50a024745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$J$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0033e22-6e20-53c9-b6ad-5502fdbe9286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ and $J$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277044} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e00ec5e-d2cb-5618-b9fc-572db656a6be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b4d40e2-069c-5e10-923d-ef1e82d45a9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-11717" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $H$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(H)=5\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(H)=0,$ we conclude that $H$ is not invertible. Since $H$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $H$ is singular.\n\n- For the matrix $J$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(J)=2\\cdot 6-3\\cdot 4=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(J)=0,$ we conclude that $J$ is not invertible. Since $J$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $J$ is singular.\n\n- The matrix $K$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\nTherefore, only $H$ and $J$ is singular."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable in a Singular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64d8056c-aeec-5a1a-9059-5f0e94f09159" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277049} {:answer/id #uuid "05dc6303-a43b-529a-a7f1-9f6d34ebdccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277050} {:answer/id #uuid "3c5f34b8-d55a-5f8e-b013-27cabcaff0f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277051} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c0fbba-f976-568b-a400-c82dd733eb64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277052} {:answer/id #uuid "83da0946-6dc3-516e-8d19-168392f39733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7c0fbba-f976-568b-a400-c82dd733eb64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277052} :answer-field/id #uuid "970a4e18-384b-538a-b9f2-9f09c076bae6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4918baf6-577a-5ac9-9070-414a1d323782" :question/math-academy-id "q-11719" :question/problem "If $F=[\\begin{aligned}3 & t \\\\ t & 3\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $F$ is singular if and only if $det(F)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(F) & =3\\cdot 3-t\\cdot t \\\\  & =9-{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =9 \\\\ t & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values of $t$ are $t=\\pm 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea23649-e835-540a-b238-7b89ed330cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5eef929-8f84-5a10-bc10-7703ce3731b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277056} {:answer/id #uuid "30e82396-a337-5593-9ff5-ddf12172f01b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277057} {:answer/id #uuid "4b130177-fd19-52cf-b01e-0d7bff394f14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277058} {:answer/id #uuid "399e29af-4845-51f4-99f1-d7a794231265" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277059} {:answer/id #uuid "70a90aa1-2980-5d4a-9665-405f8231647a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70a90aa1-2980-5d4a-9665-405f8231647a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277060} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcdd58a3-ab97-568c-b0a2-e7c8627fb096" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fd0e8ab-5ed1-5462-b099-07b3173547c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-127736" :question/problem "Calculate ${Z}^{-1}$ where\n$Z=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 8\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nZ=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $Z$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Z}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(Z)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(Z)=1\\cdot 8-3\\cdot 2=2\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Z}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(Z)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z{Z}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {Z}^{-1}Z & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea23649-e835-540a-b238-7b89ed330cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96df7422-ae24-5302-b122-de5d7da09f75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277063} {:answer/id #uuid "cd07a42e-8f00-526c-8e6b-ac348c50983a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277064} {:answer/id #uuid "ee94e148-2a0a-5c3f-82cd-3ffe92783a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277065} {:answer/id #uuid "8450a5d0-125e-5fab-98cd-1f1693cc4ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277066} {:answer/id #uuid "e77a7e5a-64db-5196-a83c-c3a82548436a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8450a5d0-125e-5fab-98cd-1f1693cc4ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277066} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c816e28-8723-5565-9497-0c57705cfeba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9cf7ac8-28c7-510c-8205-256a03a04ef2" :question/math-academy-id "q-127737" :question/problem "Calculate ${U}^{-1}$, where \n$U=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 7\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $U$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{U}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(U)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(U)=-2\\cdot 7-(-5)\\cdot 3=1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{U}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(U)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -(-5) & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U{U}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {U}^{-1}U & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea23649-e835-540a-b238-7b89ed330cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b0e4e4f-019b-54ff-a8b6-17c2dfffcb34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277070} {:answer/id #uuid "8867e674-61b8-52d5-8b4f-392b6107371a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277071} {:answer/id #uuid "3a853d67-92d5-535e-b468-8e02dad2a667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277072} {:answer/id #uuid "7c794efe-e4d2-5478-8cef-b8f0335411da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277073} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd9b462-c5f5-514f-bd5e-cd2427a802cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c794efe-e4d2-5478-8cef-b8f0335411da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277073} :answer-field/id #uuid "11f04062-ea30-5d75-a3e3-2839fb18deea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "469cce88-f2bf-5c3e-aa47-9c15a4251782" :question/math-academy-id "q-127742" :question/problem "Calculate ${B}^{-1}$, where \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $B$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(B)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(B)=2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1=-1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(B)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B{B}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {B}^{-1}B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea23649-e835-540a-b238-7b89ed330cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9c2dcc2-731d-5a2f-8829-ddb2e3ec8060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277077} {:answer/id #uuid "f136e859-7d84-5d24-a3cc-1fb3c7e2a042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277078} {:answer/id #uuid "4348962a-e8e7-54fd-be7a-7b984ea0efdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277079} {:answer/id #uuid "76426935-0289-54b1-a92e-96f7e065d446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277080} {:answer/id #uuid "ba5101f1-01d7-5fc8-a3e9-0c9b9e8225ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4348962a-e8e7-54fd-be7a-7b984ea0efdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277079} :answer-field/id #uuid "43fe747f-d987-5862-9963-de84dac167c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08bebf42-aebf-5366-9eb7-e8795f6019b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-127744" :question/problem "Calculate ${C}^{-1}$, where\n$C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $C$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(C)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(C)=3\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 3=-3\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(C)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C{C}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {C}^{-1}C & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ba5decc-4f40-528a-a242-a3d3e92808a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277084} {:answer/id #uuid "80a494ed-44d2-5724-9640-8d14f9568a20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277085} {:answer/id #uuid "10f774ec-34c7-5491-b105-7e351e4551a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277086} {:answer/id #uuid "0ab1e606-956f-5e6a-9469-fed13fe02408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277087} {:answer/id #uuid "318c1cb7-b02b-5fd4-86c9-3bba63f9bdc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ba5decc-4f40-528a-a242-a3d3e92808a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277084} :answer-field/id #uuid "2776ffed-7e53-5e1b-9126-769874216821" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb5cfafe-438d-561a-bfca-e1cc33842ff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-127755" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $B$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(B)=0\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(B)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $B$ is invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(C)=1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(C)=0,$ we conclude that $C$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $C$ do not have an inverse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98ca54b2-8431-5444-9efe-fdbd7d2615eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277091} {:answer/id #uuid "142c305f-1241-5ca4-bd3c-20f1cc8d1f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277092} {:answer/id #uuid "46e22156-77f8-53ae-b232-4c1481123bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277093} {:answer/id #uuid "2c91a010-1966-513a-b4c0-a950a36e14de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ and $S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277094} {:answer/id #uuid "231b50e7-c515-5b7a-8237-1688f362c11c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "142c305f-1241-5ca4-bd3c-20f1cc8d1f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277092} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9614a24-643b-5a1c-a444-f3d4704932cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a2fb10f-1cf2-5a6b-8b70-3549d9280b9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127757" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nS=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $S$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(S)=1\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0=-1.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(S)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $S$ is invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $T$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(T)=2\\cdot (-6)-4\\cdot (-3)=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(T)=0,$ we conclude that $T$ is not invertible. Since $T$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $T$ is singular.\n\n- For the matrix $U$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(U)=0\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (-1)=1.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(U)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $U$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $T$ is singular."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable in a Singular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46fe4e6a-5d64-59e8-b59a-19cbcaf729d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=\\pm 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277098} {:answer/id #uuid "70d152d1-86e9-5140-a55b-3ebeb9302b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277099} {:answer/id #uuid "53b2b1b1-a040-515f-8ffa-ebc582ed8323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277100} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b57769-1377-5313-9201-b4ea5d6d89ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277101} {:answer/id #uuid "6931d6c5-02bd-5e7c-908e-ef0826835da0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=\\pm 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6931d6c5-02bd-5e7c-908e-ef0826835da0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=\\pm 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277102} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3ed19da-636c-5863-a76b-57450e5b6b5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "901178dd-d45f-581f-a10a-55eab419017e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127905" :question/problem "If $P=[\\begin{aligned}n & -4 \\\\ -16 & n\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $n.$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $P$ is singular if and only if $det(P)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =n\\cdot n-(-16)\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & ={n}^{2}-64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-64 & =0 \\\\ {n}^{2} & =64 \\\\ n & =\\pm 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values of $n$ are $n=\\pm 8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea23649-e835-540a-b238-7b89ed330cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ce6153d-aa59-5359-b219-8c1c198d1afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ce6153d-aa59-5359-b219-8c1c198d1afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277105} :answer-field/id #uuid "641442f3-70be-5342-a3cc-667686c722f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277104} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d125b20c-5b8b-5ae6-bd32-8886217f5c5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d125b20c-5b8b-5ae6-bd32-8886217f5c5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277107} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b9e8a32-ea26-5050-869b-a03050ab688f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277106} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d856aa9-aea1-572e-bb1f-46b9b59fd70b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d856aa9-aea1-572e-bb1f-46b9b59fd70b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277109} :answer-field/id #uuid "4821afe3-ea54-5b82-8dda-386bf8d454c0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9dc6b8f7-0245-52df-b551-40892e2a6e3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-316780" :question/problem "Calculate ${A}^{-1},$ where\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(A)=6\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 9=-12\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-12}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1/12 & 1/6 \\\\ 3/4 & -1/2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1/12 & 1/6 \\\\ 3/4 & -1/2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {A}^{-1}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1/12 & 1/6 \\\\ 3/4 & -1/2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable in a Singular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "197273cb-fad0-550e-847c-37b81ec3acbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "197273cb-fad0-550e-847c-37b81ec3acbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277112} :answer-field/id #uuid "90f007b8-4c17-5edb-8319-70889d75f9bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "332a826c-7348-5345-899f-11bdcbd79a2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-316781" :question/problem "If $B=[\\begin{aligned}2x & 5x-1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nis a singular matrix, calculate the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $B$ is singular if and only if $det(B)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =(2x)\\cdot (-2)-4\\cdot (5x-1) \\\\  & =-4x+4-20x \\\\  & =-24x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24x+4 & =0 \\\\ 24x & =4 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{24} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08eb2ea7-f792-5e17-ba62-6cb93b9a1555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ and $Z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277115} {:answer/id #uuid "6b708952-cde1-539c-b2ec-210459186665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ and $Y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277116} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2c51de-d9e4-53e2-8df8-7c5a5dda12ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277117} {:answer/id #uuid "d7884f22-d342-599e-b966-702453ae8925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277118} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ed6083-9a87-5d3f-8c78-32388cdb4327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5ed6083-9a87-5d3f-8c78-32388cdb4327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277119} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd54c950-30a6-5bde-8785-a5144115b030" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f34f6a84-b6d1-59f1-84c8-b1742c3a5c7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71938" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 6 \\\\ 10 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,Z=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $X$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $Y$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(Y)=6\\cdot 6-7\\cdot 5=1.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(Y)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $Y$ is invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $Z$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(Z)=(-2)\\cdot 5-10\\cdot 1=-20.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(Z)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $Z$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $X$ does not have an inverse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable in a Singular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66eabc9d-f4f9-5412-a665-bb0699a45bf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277122} {:answer/id #uuid "8f632f4a-2e06-5819-a78c-9ccbe8bba4c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277123} {:answer/id #uuid "86cdccf0-1511-503b-bc55-3ff9b7747687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277124} {:answer/id #uuid "acad5599-da05-58ce-b28d-7c6e809a37a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277125} {:answer/id #uuid "4a91fe2a-3f08-55a9-b5d9-651a2576707b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f632f4a-2e06-5819-a78c-9ccbe8bba4c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277123} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b072d1b-91b4-5fae-a33a-27613fcc47a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2d15958-4ecb-5c0d-ac09-3fa2b8146b35" :question/math-academy-id "q-71967" :question/problem "If $V=[\\begin{aligned}7 & y \\\\ 5 & y\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value of $y.$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $V$ is singular if and only if $det(V)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(V) & =7\\cdot y-5\\cdot y \\\\  & =7y-5y \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $y$ is $y=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277127 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable in a Singular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a2432f1-06ef-596e-a4ad-a7b950fab294" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277129} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c77e6d-18f5-5b5d-8323-5394b92b24e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277130} {:answer/id #uuid "f965bb0f-74c0-548e-b2a8-2b56e74fca3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277131} {:answer/id #uuid "019324c7-81c8-5b53-bf56-b700abdaa7c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277132} {:answer/id #uuid "105cab17-a1b0-5700-a8de-e49a40cb8f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a2432f1-06ef-596e-a4ad-a7b950fab294" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277129} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6d60900-5445-5716-b671-a3f6295914cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02eff272-95c3-5a34-a191-db8af4578dbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-71969" :question/problem "If $M=[\\begin{aligned}t & 4 \\\\ t & 10\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $M$ is singular if and only if $det(M)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =t\\cdot 10-t\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =10t-4t \\\\  & =6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $t$ is $t=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15691103-f36a-5181-b7a7-283236c096ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277136} {:answer/id #uuid "22d4b04f-11f1-54a1-b303-31bafb35f4af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277137} {:answer/id #uuid "81a41221-0341-56ab-9673-afb161d21f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277138} {:answer/id #uuid "3952685f-e03a-542f-a8e8-f37ba5a3c54a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277139} {:answer/id #uuid "b5da8682-de33-580e-bb9b-32f89484ae5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5da8682-de33-580e-bb9b-32f89484ae5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277140} :answer-field/id #uuid "074b9c92-0e09-5b09-a3cf-2efb2a6798f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17761662-c8e1-5053-be1d-f17ca1632a52" :question/math-academy-id "q-7915" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $P$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(P)=4\\cdot 6-8\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(P)=0,$ we conclude that $P$ is not invertible. Since $P$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $P$ is singular.\n\n- For the matrix $Q$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(Q)=1\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(Q)=0,$ we conclude that $Q$ is not invertible. Since $Q$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $Q$ is singular.\n\n- The matrix $R$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\nTherefore, only $P$ and $Q$ are singular."}]}
